The entrances of the caves, called "The Seven Sisters", all have a diameter of at least 100-250 meters (328-820 feet).
Infrared images show thermal oscillations between day and night, providing that they are probably caves.
During the day they are colder than the surrounding surface and at night warmer.
Glen Cushing of the U.S. Geological Agency (USGS) team of astrogeologists and Northern Arizona University in Flagstaff, Arizona, said: "Their thermal behavior is not as constant as that of the large terrestrial caves, which often maintain a relatively stable temperature, but it's consistent with the fact that it's deep holes in the ground."
In France, voting is traditionally an untechnological experience: voters enter an isolated booth and put in an envelope a pre-printed sheet indicating the chosen candidate.
After the verification of his identity by the officials, the voter deposits the envelope in the urn and signs the electoral register.
The French electoral law codifies the procedures in a very strict way.
Since 1988 the polls must be transparent, so that voters and observers can testify that at the beginning of the ballot there are no envelopes and that there are no ones other than those of voters, duly counted and authorized.
Candidates can send their representatives to attend each stage of the process. In the evening, the volunteers carry out of the votes under strict control, in accordance with the specific procedures.
