Technological determinism is a term that includes in practice a wide range of ideas, from technological innovation or the technological imperative to the strict sense that the destiny of man is moved by an underlying logic, associated with scientific laws and their realization in technology.
Much of the interpretations of technological determinism have in common two general ideas: one is that the development of technology itself follows a path that goes far beyond political and cultural influence, and the other is that technology in turn has "effects" on societies that are intrinsic, rather than socially conditioned.
For example, we can say that the car necessarily involves the development of the road network.
However, a national road network only for a few cars is not economically viable, so new production methods are developed to reduce the costs related to the ownership of an automobile.
Mass ownership of cars also leads to a greater percentage of accidents on the roads, and consequently the invention of new rescue techniques for the care of injured people.
