Agriculture is defined as subsistency when it is practiced for the sole purpose of producing sufficient to meet the food needs of the farmer/farmer and her family.
Subsistence farming is a simple system, usually of an organic type, that uses preserved seeds originating in the ecological region in association with crop rotation or other relatively simple techniques aimed at getting the most out of crops.
Historically, most farmers practiced subsistence farming, and in many developing countries this is still the case.
Sub-cultures unite individuals with the same opinions, who do not feel represented by the standards of society, allowing them to develop a sense of identity.
Sub-cultures are distinguished by age, ethnicity, social class, position and/or sex of its members.
Characteristics that define a sub-culture as distinctive can be of a linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical nature or a set of factors.
Often members of a sub-culture indicate their belonging through a peculiar and symbolic use of style, which includes fashions, manners and argots.
One of the most commonly used methods to explain the importance of socialization is to analyze some unhappy cases of children who through negligence, as a result of misfortunes, or as a result of intentional abuses, grew up without proper socialization with adults.
These children are called "ferals" or savages. Some of them have been segregated by people (often their own parents): in some cases abandonment is motivated by the refusal of severe intellectual or physical impairment by parents.
Feral children may have suffered serious violence or trauma prior to abandonment or escape.
Other children may have been raised by animals; still others seem to have lived alone in the wilderness.
