Neurobiological results demonstrated the physical existence of a theoretical approach to cognition investigation. The area of research becomes narrower and therefore much more precise.
The link between cerebral pathology and behavior underlies scientists' research.
It has long been known that certain types of brain damage, trauma, injuries and tumors can affect behavior and modify certain mental functions.
The introduction of new technologies allows us to see and investigate structures and brain processes never previously observed.
This gives us a great deal of information and material to create simulation models that help us understand our mental processes.
Despite having a strong sci-fi connotation, AI represents a very important branch of computing that deals with behavior, learning and intelligent adaptation of machines.
AI research includes making machines to automate operations that require intelligent behavior.
Some application examples are control, planning and programming, the ability to respond to customer diagnoses and questions, as well as writing, voice and facial recognition.
These things have become distinct disciplines, focusing on providing solutions to real problems.
The AI system is now often used in the economic, medical, engineering and military fields, as well as being integrated into several applications software for home computers and video games.
Trips represent a fundamental part of teaching. Often teachers would like to take their students to places that you can’t reach by bus.
The solution is offered by technology with virtual trips. Students can see the artefacts of the museums, visit an aquarium or admire beautiful works of art sitting in the classroom.
Sharing a virtual school trip is also an excellent pretext to share experiences and travel reflections with future classes.
For example, every year Bennet School students, North Carolina, design a website about their trip to the capital; the website is redesigned every year, but previous versions are stored online and become an album.
Blogs can also help to improve the writing of students. Often, in fact, their first experiences with blogs are characterized by an undertreated grammar and spelling, but generally the presence of an audience changes things.
Since students are usually the most critical audience, to avoid negative judgments the author of the blog is committed to improving writing.
Blogs also “force students to become more aware of the moment around them”. The need to nurture the interest of the public motivates students to be ingenious and interesting (Toto, 2004).
The blog is a tool that aims to encourage collaboration and develop student learning well beyond the normal school day.
Correct use of blogs “can help students become more critical and analytical. By actively responding to Internet materials, students can define their position within the writings of others, as well as outlining their perspectives on specific issues” (Oravec, 2002).
Ottawa is Canada’s evocative and bilingual capital, full of art galleries and museums chronicling the country’s past and present.
Further south are the Niagara Falls and to the north you can admire the natural and unspoiled beauty of the Muskoka region and much more.
All of this and more makes Ontario typically Canadian according to the views of foreigners.
The large areas further north are sparsely populated, among them some are almost uninhabited wild lands.
If you make a comparison of the population, the result can be surprising for many: in the United States the number of African-American citizens is higher than that of Canadian citizens.
East African islands are located in the Indian Ocean, off the east coast of Africa.
Madagascar is by far the largest and its natural heritage makes it a continent in its own right.
Almost all of the smaller islands, known as luxurious seaside resorts, are mostly independent nations or associated with France.
In the land the Arabs also brought Islam, which undertook a long journey to the Comoros and Mayotte.
In the 15th century, the discovery of the Cape route, from Europe to India, by Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama paved the way for European influence and colonialism.
To the north, the region is bordered by Sahel, while to the south and west is bordered by the Atlantic Ocean.
For female travelers: it is recommended for everyone to declare to be married, regardless of the actual marital status.
It is also useful to wear a ring (but not one that looks too expensive).
It is appropriate for women to realize that cultural differences can give rise to what they consider harassment, and that it is not unusual to be followed, grasped by the arm, etc.
Have the firmness of rejecting men, and don’t be afraid to object (cultural differences or not, there is no justification!).
The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BC; under the name of Anfa, it was a port of strategic importance used by the Phoenicians, Romans and Merenids.
The Portuguese destroyed it to reconstruct it with the name of Casa Branca, which they abandoned in 1755 following an earthquake.
The Moroccan sultan rededified the city named Daru l-Badya, then named Casablanca by the Spanish merchants who chose these places for their commercial bases.
Across Morocco, Casablanca is one of the less interesting cities for shopping.
In the surroundings of the old Medina it is easy to find places that sell traditional Moroccan products, such as tajins, ceramics, leather goods, narghilè and a lot of chincaglieria, but all this is reserved only for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the easternmost part of the Democratic Republic of Congo, not far from Rwanda.
In 2002, Nyiragongo's lava destroyed Goma, burying much of the city's streets, especially those in the center.
While Goma is reasonably safe, it is appropriate to document oneself well before carrying out any visits outside of Goma, so as to well assess the status of the clashes that still persist in the province of North Kivu.
The city is also the starting point for climbing to the Nyiragongo volcano, as well as being one of the least expensive opportunities to see Africa's mountain gorillas.
You can use a boda-boda (taxi-motorcyclet) to move to Goma. The normal price (in local currency) is about 500 Congolese francs for a short ride.
Even as a consequence of its relative inaccessibility, "Timbuktù" ended up becoming a metaphor for exotic and distant land.
Today Timbuktu is a fallen city, but due to its reputation it remains a tourist attraction, also equipped with an airport.
In 1990 it was included in the list of Unesco heritage sites that are endangered, due to the threat posed by desert sands.
In PBS's Henry Louis Gates "Wonders of the African Words" ("Wonders of the African World") was one of the main stages.
With its more arabic than African touch, the city stands out clearly from the others in the country.
Kruger (Kruger National Park, KNP) is located north-east of South Africa and winds east along the border with Mozambique, to the north borders Zimbabwe, and its southern border is the Crocodile River.
With an area of 19,500 km2, the park includes 14 different ecozones, each of which gives sustenance to a different wildlife.
It is one of South Africa's main attractions and is considered to be the most important of the South African national parks (SANParks).
As for all the national parks of South Africa, there are daily contributions for storage and entry to the park.
Purchasing a Wild Card may also be useful, as it allows access to a selection of parks in South Africa or all South African national parks.
Hong Kong Island, which gives its name to the entire territory, is the main destination for many tourists.
The series of buildings forming Hong Kong's skyline has been compared to a glittering bar chart, even more noticeable because framed by the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To enjoy the best views of Hong Kong, leave the island and visit Kowloon’s waterfront opposite.
Most of Hong Kong island's urban development focused on reclaimed land on the densely populated northern coast.
This is the place the English colonizers chose for themselves, so if you are looking for testimonies of the colonial past of this land, this is a good point from which to start.
Sundarbans are the world's largest swath of mangrovias. It stretches for 80 km (50 mi) from the hinterland coast of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarbans have been declared a World Heritage Site by UNESCO. The part of forest included in Indian territory is called Sundarbans National Park.
However, forests are not only made up of mangrove marshes: in them there is also what is left of the imposing jungles that once covered the plain of the Ganges.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km2, of which about a third consists of swampy areas.
Since 1966 the Sundarbans have become a sanctuary for wildlife; in the area it is estimated the presence of 400 royal tigers from Bengal and about 30,000 maculate deer.
Buses leave from the interdistrectual bus station (on the other side of the river) throughout the day, although many (especially those heading east and Jakar/Bumthang) leave between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since often the service buses between different districts are full, it will be good to buy the ticket a few days in advance.
Most neighbourhoods are reached by comfortable and sturdy small Japanese buses.
Shared taxis are a convenient and fast means to reach nearby locations, including Paro (150 Nu) and Punakha (200 Nu).
The bridge over the Oyapock River is a staunched bridge connecting the city of Oiapoque, Brazil, in Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
Its two pylons have a height of 83 meters, is 378 meters long and has two lanes 3.50 meters wide.
Under the bridge the free vertical space is 15 meters. The construction ended in August 2011, but the opening to traffic took place only in March 2017.
In September 2017, when Brazilian customs checkpoints are likely completed, the bridge is expected to be totally functional.
The Guaranís were the most important indigenous group living in present-day eastern Paraguay. They were semi-nomadic hunters and also practiced subsistence farming.
The Chaco region was inhabited by other groups of indigenous tribes such as the Guaycuru and Payaguá, who lived by hunting, gathering and fishing.
Paraguay, formerly called "The Great Province of the Indies", was born in the sixteenth century from the encounter between the Spanish conquerors and native indigenous groups.
The Spaniards began a period that lasted three centuries, the period of colonization.
Since the founding of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay has been able to maintain much of its identity and its native peculiarities.
Everyone knows that Argentina boasts one of the best teams and the best polo players in the world.
In December, the most important tournament of the year takes place in Las Cañitas polo fields.
Here you can attend minor tournaments and matches even at other times of the year.
To receive information about tournaments and the purchase of tickets for polo matches, please contact Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
Falklands have their own official coin (FAlkland pound), the value of which is equivalent to that of the British pound (GBP).
The only bank of the islands, located in Stanley in front of the FIC West store, offers currency exchange service.
British pounds are typically accepted anywhere on the islands. At Stanley you can often also pay with U.S. credit cards and dollars.
It is likely that on the outlying islands do not accept credit cards, although British and U.S. currencies can be accepted. Check in advance with the owners to decide an acceptable payment method.
It is almost impossible to trade the Falkland currency outside the islands, so change the money before leaving.
Since Montevideo is south of the Equator, it's summer when in the northern hemisphere it's winter and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in the subtropical region; during the summer months the temperatures over 30 ° C are quite normal.
Winter can be deceptively cold: temperatures rarely reach below zero, but the combination of wind and humidity gives the feeling of a temperature lower than that marked by the thermometer.
There are no "dry" and "rainy" seasons defined: the amount of rain remains almost constant throughout the year.
Although many of the park's animals are used to the sight of man, the fauna is still wild and should not be fed or disturbed.
Park authorities recommend maintaining at least a distance of at least 100 yards/meters from bears and wolves and a distance of 25 yards/meters from all other wild animals.
No matter how docile they may seem: bison, elk, deer, bears, virtually all large animals can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors suffer wounds for not maintaining an adequate distance. It is necessary to leave these animals an adequate space since they are large, savage and potentially dangerous animals.
Also keep in mind that odors can attract bears and other wild animals, so don’t bring with you or cooking foods with very strong odors, and try to keep the camp clean.
Apia is the capital of the Samoan Islands. It is located on the island of Upolu and its population has just under 40,000 inhabitants.
Apia was founded between 1850 and 1860 and is the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
In this port occurred the sadly known naval deadlock of 1889, in which seven ships from Germany, the United States and Britain refused to abandon the port.
All ships, with the exception of a British cruiser, were sunk. Nearly 200 U.S. and German lives were lost.
In the course of the struggle for independence organized by the Mau movement, a peaceful rally in the city led to the killing of Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III, the supreme leader.
There are many beaches, since Auckland is located between two ports. The most popular are divided into three areas.
Beaches overlooking the North Shore's Pacific Ocean (in North Harbour District) stretch from Long Bay to north to Devonport to the south.
These are mainly sandy beaches, safe for swimming, and most of them enjoy the shade of pohutukawa trees.
Tamaki Drive beaches in Auckland Central, in the elegant suburbs of Mission Bay and St Heliers, overlook the Port of Waitemata.
These are family-friended beaches sometimes a little crowded, surrounded by a wide choice of shops. You can swim safely.
The most well-known local beer is an uncomplex, pleasant and refreshing beer called "Number One." The other local beer is called "Manta."
You can have many French wines, but Australian and New Zealand wines could face the trip better.
Local faucet water is totally safe to drink, but if you're afraid, bottled water is easy to find.
According to Australians, 'flat white' coffee comes from a foreign idea. 'Espresso' is black short coffee, cappuccino is covered with cream (not foam) and tea is served without milk.
Hot chocolate is at the same level as the Belgian one. Fruit juices are expensive, but very good.
